# PART E — MICROPHONE AND CONVERSATION

## 10. LIVE HUMAN VOICE INPUT

Implement microphone input so the human can speak naturally to the AI opponents.

This is a core requirement, not an optional feature.

### User interaction

Display a small microphone icon near the bottom of the screen.

Use push-to-talk interaction.

The user should be able to:

1. Press the microphone button.
2. Speak into their microphone.
3. Have their speech converted into text.
4. Release the microphone button.
5. Send the transcript to the backend.
6. Receive natural responses from one or more AI opponents.

The user must be able to use the microphone during the game, not only during their own turn.

### Speech-to-text implementation

For the MVP, prefer browser-native speech recognition when available.

Use the browser SpeechRecognition / webkitSpeechRecognition interface behind a small service abstraction.

Handle browser compatibility gracefully.

Provide an alternative text-input field if speech recognition is unavailable or permission is denied.

If necessary, add a separate speech-to-text backend implementation later.

Do not make the project dependent on a paid speech-recognition API unless absolutely required.

### Important privacy behaviour

- Request microphone permission explicitly.
- Only listen while the user deliberately activates the microphone.
- Show a clear recording indicator.
- Stop capture when the user releases the button.
- Do not silently record the user.
- Do not store raw audio.
- Keep transcripts within the game session unless the user explicitly requests otherwise.
- Do not expose raw audio or private transcripts unnecessarily.

### Transcription feedback

Show a short message containing the recognized speech.

For example:

Human: "I think you've got nothing."

This lets the player verify that the microphone captured the intended statement.

### Voice input is conversation, not a game command

Spoken dialogue should not automatically trigger poker actions.

For example, saying "I call your bluff" should not automatically submit a Call action.

The player must use the normal action controls to make official poker moves.

This prevents accidental actions caused by speech recognition errors.

